# 9. SLI vs SLO vs SLA ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

## SLI — Service Level Indicator

A measurable indicator of service behavior.

Example:

```text
Successful Tracking Requests
────────────────────────────
Total Eligible Tracking Requests
```

## SLO — Service Level Objective

The target for an SLI.

Example:

```text
Tracking API Availability
          ≥ 99.9%
```

over a defined measurement window.

## SLA — Service Level Agreement

A formal service commitment that may include consequences when targets are not met.

### Example MLflow SLOs

These are illustrative targets, not MLflow defaults.

| Capability | Example SLO |
|---|---|
| Tracking API | 99.9% success |
| Registry lookup | 99.9% success |
| Artifact downloads | 99.9% success |
| Registry latency | 99% under 500 ms |
| Run creation | 99.9% success |

### Mental Model

```text
SLI
 ↓
What are we measuring?

SLO
 ↓
What target do we want?

SLA
 ↓
What formal commitment exists?
```

---

# 10. Error Budgets ⭐⭐⭐⭐

Suppose we define:

```text
Availability SLO = 99.9%
Measurement Window = 30 Days
```

The corresponding time-based unavailability allowance is:

```text
30 days = 43,200 minutes

Allowed Unavailability
= 43,200 × 0.001

= 43.2 minutes
```

This is the time-based equivalent of a 99.9% availability objective.

For a request-based SLO, the error budget is instead measured using eligible requests.

### Why Error Budgets Matter

If the platform consumes most of its error budget:

- Investigate incidents.
- Review reliability risks.
- Prioritize corrective actions.
- Reconsider risky deployments.
- Apply the organization's error-budget policy.

### Mental Model

```text
SLO
  ↓
Reliability Target
  ↓
Error Budget
  ↓
Allowed Failure Within Window
```

